{# One labelled box, written once, in DaisyUI's form components.

   Every admin form used to spell out its own `<label for>`, control, hint and
   error, roughly seventy-five times across sixteen templates, against a class
   vocabulary each template had to remember. That is how /admin/users drifted
   into hidden labels nobody else used (TASK-97): a field written by hand can
   forget the error line, leave a label unbound, or invent a class the
   stylesheet has never heard of, and nothing says it is wrong.

   Each macro here emits one `fieldset` holding the same four things in the
   same order:

     1. the `<label for>`, bound to the control by its id, drawn as the
        field's `fieldset-legend`,
     2. the control: `input`, `textarea`, `select` or `checkbox`, small, the
        width of its column, and a `validator` when the form refused it,
     3. the field error, where the form has something to say about this box,
        as the `validator-hint` that a refused control turns red,
     4. the hint, where the field needs explaining, as a `label`.

   The `fieldset` is a class on a `<div>`, as DaisyUI's own validator example
   puts it on a `<label>` and a `<form>`: a `<fieldset>` element is a group
   that a screen reader names by its legend, and one box has no group to name.
   The label is a `<label>` carrying `fieldset-legend` rather than a
   `<legend>`, because a legend names a group and not the control in it.

   Error before hint, because that is the order every screen already used: the
   sentence about what went wrong belongs next to the box it is about, and the
   standing explanation sits under both.

   This file is under the admin directory, which is the admin environment's
   only search root (`src/admin/templates.ts`). A site's theme can override
   any public template and cannot reach in here, so no theme can shadow or
   break a field on the login form.

   `label` and `hint` are written into the page as markup, because the admin's
   labels and hints carry `<code>`, `<em>` and links. They are template
   literals, never anything a visitor typed; a hint that interpolates a value
   should be captured with `{% set … %}…{% endset %}`, which escapes what it
   interpolates before this ever sees it. `error` is a message from the server
   and is escaped. #}

{% from "components/alert.njk" import alert %}

{# The field error, then the hint: the two paragraphs every macro below ends
   with. The error is a `validator-hint`, which DaisyUI shows only after a
   control marked `aria-invalid` with the `validator` class, so it has to stay
   that control's later sibling. `errorHtml` is the rare second thing a field
   has to say — the Reading page's "that page is no longer published" —
   already written as its own paragraph, and drawn here in the error colour
   whether or not the form was refused. The hint is a `label` laid out as a
   block, since an inline flex row would split a sentence around its `<code>`,
   and wrapped, since DaisyUI's label does not wrap. #}
{% macro _below(id, hint, error, errorHtml) %}
{% if error %}
  <p class="validator-hint" id="{{ id }}-error">{{ error }}</p>
{% endif %}
{% if errorHtml %}
  <div class="text-error">{{ errorHtml | safe }}</div>
{% endif %}
{% if hint %}
  <p class="label block whitespace-normal">{{ hint | safe }}</p>
{% endif %}
{% endmacro %}

{# What a control says about itself when the form refused it: that it is
   invalid, and which paragraph says why, so a screen reader reads the message
   with the field rather than leaving it to be found. `errorHtml` is not named
   here: it is a standing note about the stored value, not a refusal. #}
{% macro _invalid(id, error) -%}
{% if error %} aria-invalid="true" aria-describedby="{{ id }}-error"{% endif %}
{%- endmacro %}

{# A box somebody types into: text, email, number, or whatever `type` says.

   `value` is always written, even empty, so the control's state is on the page
   rather than implied by its absence. A read-only box is drawn on the darker
   base, as DaisyUI draws a disabled one, and with a dashed edge, since a dark
   theme's two bases are close, so it does not look like a box that takes
   typing; its text stays at full strength, since it is there to be read and
   copied. #}
{% macro text(
  id,
  name,
  label,
  value='',
  type='text',
  hint='',
  error='',
  errorHtml='',
  min='',
  step='',
  inputmode='',
  autocomplete='',
  placeholder='',
  spellcheck='',
  required=false,
  autofocus=false,
  readonly=false,
  disabled=false
) %}
<div class="fieldset">
  <label class="fieldset-legend" for="{{ id }}">{{ label | safe }}</label>
  <input id="{{ id }}" name="{{ name }}" type="{{ type }}"{% if min %} min="{{ min }}"{% endif %}{% if step %} step="{{ step }}"{% endif %}{% if inputmode %} inputmode="{{ inputmode }}"{% endif %}{% if autocomplete %} autocomplete="{{ autocomplete }}"{% endif %} value="{{ value }}"{% if placeholder %} placeholder="{{ placeholder }}"{% endif %}{% if spellcheck %} spellcheck="{{ spellcheck }}"{% endif %}{% if required %} required{% endif %}{% if autofocus %} autofocus{% endif %}{% if readonly %} readonly{% endif %}{% if disabled %} disabled{% endif %}{{ _invalid(id, error) }} class="input input-sm w-full read-only:bg-base-200 read-only:border-dashed{% if error %} validator{% endif %}" />
{{ _below(id, hint, error, errorHtml) }}
</div>
{%- endmacro %}

{# A password box. It carries `value=""` and no way to set one: nothing typed
   into a password field is ever printed back into the page, and a stored
   secret is never printed at all — the placeholder says whether there is one.
   #}
{% macro password(
  id,
  name,
  label,
  hint='',
  error='',
  errorHtml='',
  autocomplete='',
  placeholder='',
  spellcheck='',
  required=false,
  autofocus=false
) %}
<div class="fieldset">
  <label class="fieldset-legend" for="{{ id }}">{{ label | safe }}</label>
  <input id="{{ id }}" name="{{ name }}" type="password" value=""{% if autocomplete %} autocomplete="{{ autocomplete }}"{% endif %}{% if spellcheck %} spellcheck="{{ spellcheck }}"{% endif %}{% if placeholder %} placeholder="{{ placeholder }}"{% endif %}{% if required %} required{% endif %}{% if autofocus %} autofocus{% endif %}{{ _invalid(id, error) }} class="input input-sm w-full{% if error %} validator{% endif %}" />
{{ _below(id, hint, error, errorHtml) }}
</div>
{%- endmacro %}

{# A box of several lines, read-only drawn as the text box draws it. #}
{% macro textarea(
  id,
  name,
  label,
  value='',
  hint='',
  error='',
  errorHtml='',
  rows='',
  spellcheck='',
  required=false,
  autofocus=false,
  readonly=false,
  disabled=false
) %}
<div class="fieldset">
  <label class="fieldset-legend" for="{{ id }}">{{ label | safe }}</label>
  <textarea id="{{ id }}" name="{{ name }}"{% if rows %} rows="{{ rows }}"{% endif %}{% if spellcheck %} spellcheck="{{ spellcheck }}"{% endif %}{% if required %} required{% endif %}{% if autofocus %} autofocus{% endif %}{% if readonly %} readonly{% endif %}{% if disabled %} disabled{% endif %}{{ _invalid(id, error) }} class="textarea textarea-sm w-full read-only:bg-base-200 read-only:border-dashed{% if error %} validator{% endif %}">{{ value }}</textarea>
{{ _below(id, hint, error, errorHtml) }}
</div>
{%- endmacro %}

{# A list to pick from. The options are the call's body, because which of them
   is `selected` is the caller's business and an option's label is markup as
   often as it is a word:

     {% call field.select('settings-homepage', fields.homepage, 'Homepage') %}
       <option value="">Your latest posts</option>
     {% endcall %} #}
{% macro select(
  id,
  name,
  label,
  hint='',
  error='',
  errorHtml='',
  required=false,
  autofocus=false,
  disabled=false
) %}
<div class="fieldset">
  <label class="fieldset-legend" for="{{ id }}">{{ label | safe }}</label>
  <select id="{{ id }}" name="{{ name }}"{% if required %} required{% endif %}{% if autofocus %} autofocus{% endif %}{% if disabled %} disabled{% endif %}{{ _invalid(id, error) }} class="select select-sm w-full{% if error %} validator{% endif %}">
{{ caller() }}
  </select>
{{ _below(id, hint, error, errorHtml) }}
</div>
{%- endmacro %}

{# A switch: the tick inside its label with the sentence beside it, as
   DaisyUI draws a checkbox, so the whole line toggles it. The sentence is in
   the text colour rather than a label's muted one, because it is what the
   tick means rather than a note about it, and it wraps. A refused box puts
   the `validator` on the label, whose later sibling the error is. #}
{% macro checkbox(
  id,
  name,
  label,
  checked=false,
  value='1',
  hint='',
  error='',
  errorHtml='',
  disabled=false
) %}
<div class="fieldset">
  <label class="label whitespace-normal text-base-content{% if error %} validator{% endif %}" for="{{ id }}">
    <input id="{{ id }}" name="{{ name }}" type="checkbox" value="{{ value }}"{% if checked %} checked{% endif %}{% if disabled %} disabled{% endif %}{{ _invalid(id, error) }} class="checkbox checkbox-sm" />
    {{ label | safe }}
  </label>
{{ _below(id, hint, error, errorHtml) }}
</div>
{%- endmacro %}

{# The top of a refused form: what went wrong, and a link to every field that
   needs putting right. It is an error alert and takes focus when the page
   loads, so a screen reader hears it at once and a keyboard starts from it,
   with no script. It comes before the form, so its `autofocus` is the first
   on the page and wins over any a field carries.

   A form with one thing to say calls it bare, with the message as the heading.
   A form with fields to fix calls it with a `problem` per field; one given no
   message writes nothing, so a page lists every field it could fault:

     {% call field.summary('Nothing was saved. Fix the fields marked below.') %}
       {{ field.problem('settings-title', problems.title) }}
     {% endcall %}

   Only one form on a page is ever refused at a time, which is why the ids are
   fixed. #}
{% macro summary(heading) %}
{% set problems = (caller() if caller else '') | trim %}
{% call alert(color='error', labelledby='admin-error-summary-heading', focus=true) %}
  <div>
    <h2 id="admin-error-summary-heading" class="font-semibold">{{ heading }}</h2>
{% if problems %}
    <ul class="list-disc ps-5">
{{ problems | safe }}
    </ul>
{% endif %}
  </div>
{% endcall %}
{%- endmacro %}

{# One field's line in the summary: its message, linking to the field. #}
{% macro problem(id, message) %}
{% if message %}
      <li><a class="link" href="#{{ id }}">{{ message }}</a></li>
{% endif %}
{%- endmacro %}
